A||||5||saleenfiero||Raced an '87 SE yesterday||||1||
Z||000000||saleenfiero||06-05-2001||08:35 PM||||Riddle me this....<BR>Rolled up next to my new friend in his '87 SE V6 5-speed with compucam, K&N and some kind of ignition coil do up.  I'm in my stocker '86 auto 2.8 with nothing but a K&N, we let er rip and I kept right up with him.  He shifted good and hard, but never got more than a foot on me from which my car shifted and kept right up with him until we shut er down.  Now, do I have some kind of mod in my car that I might not know about or does these upgrades to this guys car (which I saw) not do all that much.<P>Shoot, shoot me some info||||reg||1
Z||000001||88formula||06-05-2001||08:43 PM||||There are all kinds of factors that could have attributed to this like driver skill and motor condition/mileage. If he has the Crane2030 in a little 2.8-liter engine than he is going to make less torque than you have and if it were a short 0-60 mph race than he may not beat you by much.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||06-05-2001||09:24 PM||||I'm thinking that driver skill may have played a large part. Yeah, if the cam has dropped off his bottom end, then he may need to shift higher than what he'd normally do. He may actually run up against the rev-limiter before hitting his peak power. <BR>Stock, the 5-speed <I>should</I> blow the auto out of the water, off the line, and in the dust. I've driven both, and own one. The stick feels faster, and runs faster. The auto is not gear-friendly  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><BR>If you got the chance to shift, then you probably had it up to around 80-90kph, so more than enough time to let that cam kick in. I'd say that he/she hasn't mastered the car yet, or there's something wrong with it.<BR>Just my 0.00345c (.02c CDN)<P>||||reg||7
Z||000003||Mach10||06-05-2001||09:24 PM||||*DIE EVIL CLONE, DIE!!*<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 06-05-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000004||saleenfiero||06-06-2001||11:07 AM||||He didn't specify what kind of cam he had, but I really couldn't figure out why he put one in.  So most cam upgrades on a stocker would not produce much low end?  Is that what you guys are saying?||||reg||1
Z||000005||Scott||06-06-2001||02:04 PM||||This is OT, but related... A friend and I cooincidentally both owned '90 Yamaha FJ1200s.  His was modified with a V&H ignition (like an MSD), StageIII jet kit, K&Ns for each carb, and Supertrapp pipe; mine was stock 'cept for a stock fitting K&N.  (neither bike had ram air & they both had 'bout the same milage- even both had recent valve adjusts)  I considered them equal 'cept for mods and riders.  Get this: whenever we raced , we were always near dead even, until approaching top speed, when he would begin to pull away.  We switched bikes and found the same thing occured.  <BR>  I've seen similar circumstances with other vehicles.  I'm not saying that performace modifications don't improve HP and torque, but I don't think adding certain modifications to a relatively small displacement motor like a 2.8 is going to provide that much of a street performance difference.  Of course, this is only IMHO, and doesn't include displacement mods, nitrous, and/or turbos.    <BR>||||reg||1
